<h2>Why Metro Detroit map packs punish sloppy profiles</h2>
<p>When someone searches “plumber near me” in Warren or “salon Royal Oak,” Google blends website signals, review velocity, and Business Profile accuracy. A perfect site with a mismatched category or a hidden address policy will still lose to a competitor whose phone number matches everywhere. I set up profiles on every Webstar build; this checklist is what I walk Michigan clients through after staging review.</p>
<p>Metro Detroit spans multiple counties — Wayne, Oakland, Macomb — and customers describe places by corridor (Woodward, Hall Road, Van Dyke) as often as by city. Your profile should reflect how you actually serve people, not how you wish Google would draw a radius.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Claim or create the right listing</h2>
<p>Search your exact business name and address on Google Maps. If a gray or unclaimed pin exists, claim it. If a duplicate exists from an old location, request merge or mark closed after you document the move. Home-based and service-area businesses must follow Google’s address visibility rules — do not publish a fake suite to look downtown.</p>
<p>Use one Google account you control long term — ideally a company Gmail or Workspace login, not an employee who might leave. Add me as manager during setup if you want help, then keep primary ownership.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Primary and secondary categories</h2>
<p>Pick the single category that matches revenue. A HVAC company is not a “general contractor” just because you sometimes hang drywall. Secondary categories cover add-ons: “Furnace repair service,” “Air conditioning contractor,” etc. Salon businesses split hair vs nail categories carefully so map filters stay accurate.</p>
<p>Categories affect which searches you compete in. Changing them without strategy can drop impressions for a week while Google re-learns — note your baseline in Search Console before big edits.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Service area vs storefront</h2>
<p>Storefronts display address and hours tied to foot traffic — think downtown Birmingham retail. Service-area businesses hide the home address but list cities or ZIPs they cover. Be honest: if you won’t cross 8 Mile for less than a minimum job, your service list should not pretend otherwise. Customers who feel misled leave one-star reviews.</p>
<ul>
<li>Align service cities with your website footer and contact page</li>
<li>Use the same business name spelling on trucks, invoices, and social headers</li>
<li>Update holiday hours on both site and profile before Thanksgiving and Art on the Avenue weekends</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 4: Phone, website, and appointment links</h2>
<p>Use a tracked main line you actually answer — not a personal cell that goes to voicemail during jobs. Website URL must be HTTPS and match the canonical domain on your Webstar site. Appointment links should land on your booking page, not a generic Facebook scheduler unless that is truly where you want leads.</p>
<p>Click-to-call from mobile maps is huge for trades. Test it yourself on an Android on LTE, not just Wi-Fi in your office.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Description and services list</h2>
<p>Write a plain-language description: who you help, where, and one differentiator. Skip keyword stuffing; Google reads it like a human. Use the Services section for named offerings with prices if your market expects it — many Detroit home-service clients appreciate “water heater install — call for quote” clarity.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Photos on a schedule</h2>
<p>Upload real team, vehicle, and job photos monthly. Stock images of smiling models in hard hats hurt trust. Geotagging is optional; authenticity is not. Before/after sets work for cleaners and landscapers; interior shots help restaurants and salons.</p>
<p>Blur license plates or customer faces when privacy matters. Michigan winter shots prove you operate year-round — valuable for ice-fishing guides and snow removal alike without naming any single client case study here.</p>

<h2>Step 7: Hours, special hours, and attributes</h2>
<p>Set regular hours, then add special hours for holidays before shoppers check “Open now.” Attributes — women-owned, veteran-led, wheelchair access — only apply when true. Mislabeled attributes are reportable by competitors.</p>

<h2>Step 8: Verification and reviews foundation</h2>
<p>Complete Google verification promptly — postcard, phone, or email depending on what Google offers. Until verified, visibility is limited. Pair profile launch with a review plan (see our first-ten-reviews guide) instead of buying fake stars.</p>

<h2>Step 9: Match your website NAP</h2>
<p>Name, address, phone on your homepage footer should mirror Google exactly. If you rebrand, update both the same week. Mixed “LLC” suffixes confuse crawlers and humans.</p>

<h2>Step 10: Post-launch checks I run on Webstar sites</h2>
<p>Link from site header or footer to directions when storefront. Embed a map only if it helps; service-area businesses often skip map embeds. Confirm Analytics tracks call clicks and form submits. Search Console should show your domain, not a staging subdomain.</p>
<p>Metro Detroit competition is local — your profile and site are one system. Run this checklist once at launch, then quarterly when seasons change. If you want setup done with your $350–$500 Simple Website, intake includes Google walkthrough; you keep every account.</p>
<h2>Duplicate listings and moves across counties</h2>
<p>Moving from Ferndale to a Warren industrial suite trips many businesses. Update address only when Google allows visibility for your model, or close the old pin after the move. Running two active listings splits reviews — merge when possible. Document forwarding address for postcard verification if needed.</p>
<p>Franchise and DBA name changes need coordinated updates on EIN paperwork, signage, website h1, and profile title. A week of mismatch costs calls.</p>

<h2>Metro Detroit neighborhood language on-page</h2>
<p>Customers search “near Somerset” or “by Little Caesars Arena” even when the city field says Detroit. Mention landmarks only when truthful. Service-area businesses list cities, not fake storefronts. Your Webstar site footer should use the same city list for crawl consistency.</p>

<h2>Posts, Q&amp;A, and messaging</h2>
<p>Google posts announce sales, closures, and hires — short updates with a photo outperform text alone. Q&amp;A on the profile should be monitored; seed common questions if empty. Turn on messaging only if someone will reply within minutes during business hours; stale chat hurts trust.</p>

<h2>Reporting you should glance at monthly</h2>
<p>Insights show discovery searches and call clicks. Sudden drops may mean a category change, competitor surge, or website downtime. Pair with Search Console impressions for your brand name. Standard Care on WaaS or Care Plans includes a plain-English summary so you do not live inside dashboards.</p>

<h2>When to hire help vs DIY tweaks</h2>
<p>Owners can update hours and photos themselves after I document steps at launch. Category overhauls, multi-location strategy, or reinstatement after suspension benefit from experienced setup — included on Webstar packages at $350–$500 and above. Do not gamble manager access on a marketing intern’s personal Gmail.</p>
<h2>Coordination with a new Webstar website</h2>
<p>During build I use staging URLs that should not appear on Google until launch. On go-live day, swap the profile website field to production and request re-indexing in Search Console. UTM parameters on the profile link are optional; consistency matters more than tracking gymnastics on day one.</p>
<p>If your Simple Website at $350–$500 includes only three pages, ensure the homepage summarizes services Google lists in your profile categories. Mismatch triggers lower conversion even when impressions look fine.</p>
<h2>Quick recap for busy owners</h2>
<p>You do not need a marketing degree to execute this — you need a consistent process tied to jobs you already finish. Block thirty minutes on your calendar after launch week to walk the steps above with whoever answers your phone. Revisit the list each quarter when Michigan seasons change demand, hours, and photo opportunities. If anything here overlaps with paid packages, it is because Webstar builds sites and Google setup together so you are not juggling five vendors with conflicting advice.</p>
